The Mojo compiler for Dolly programs and WebGPU kernels.
Against github.com/modular/modular at the tag mojo/v1.1.0; apply with patch -p1.

bazel/public-patches/llvm_project.bzl
  The compiler links the LLVM backends of this list; WebAssembly was not one.
Support/include/Support/MDialect/MAttrs.td
  A target's OS is named through LLVM's enumeration, where Dolly is "unknown".
  The standard library chooses its libc bindings by this name.
Support/lib/MArchTarget/MArchTargetMinimal.cpp
  The CPU name "generic" was looked up as an AArch64 CPU for every triple, so
  a wasm64 target got +neon, +fp-armv8 and +ete and LLVM warned of each.
  A Dolly target has atomics and exception-handling beyond that CPU's
  features, as every object of Dolly's C compiler has: the linker refuses to
  share memory with an object without atomics. It also has tail-call: a
  coroutine resumes through a call that must be a tail call, and without the
  feature LLVM fails every async function.
Mojo/lib/Compiler/ObjectCompiler/MCLinker.cpp
  Code is generated in one MC context and printed in another. Wasm code
  names its function table by a symbol of the first, the printer makes the
  symbol again in the second, and the object declared
  __indirect_function_table twice: wasm-ld refused every program with both an
  indirect call and a function pointer.
Mojo/lib/Target/Dolly, Mojo/lib/KGENToLLVM/Target/Dolly,
Mojo/lib/Compiler/ObjectCompiler/Target/Dolly (new)
  Two targets, each registered as upstream's host target is:
  wasm64-unknown-dolly, a program: an object for Dolly's linker, generated as
  wasm64-unknown-emscripten, the triple of Dolly's libc archives;
  wasm32-unknown-webgpu, a kernel: compile_info's "asm" is WGSL text.

